1. Executive Summary
During a white-box assessment within the internal network, I successfully intercepted user authentication hashes by exploiting the LLMNR (Link-Local Multicast Name Resolution) and NBT-NS protocols. (Note: It's a laboratory I created.)

Critical Note: The captured NetNTLMv2 hashes are highly susceptible to offline brute-force attacks using tools like Hashcat or John the Ripper (JTR). If the target user utilizes a weak or common password, the plain-text credentials can be recovered in a matter of seconds, leading to a full account compromise and potential lateral movement within the Active Directory environment.

2. Methodology
Step 1: Network Poisoning (Passive/Active Listening)
Using Responder.py, I placed my machine in "Listen" mode to intercept multicast and broadcast queries on the local network segment.

Bash
sudo responder -I eth0 -wd
Step 2: Triggering the Authentication (The "Quick Access" Vector)
From the victim machine (Alejandro1), I simulated a common user error: attempting to access a non-existent network share through Windows File Explorer.

Input Path: \\SQLserver\hi

Since the local DNS server cannot resolve the hostname "SQLserver," the victim machine falls back to LLMNR/NBT-NS and broadcasts a request to the entire network. Responder intercepted this request and spoofed the identity of the target server, forcing the client to send its NetNTLMv2 authentication hash to my attacker machine.

Step 3: Captured Evidence
The following hash was successfully extracted from the network traffic:

Service: [SMB] NTLMv2-SSP

Username: ALEJANDRO\Alejandro1

Target SPN: cifs/SQLserver

Captured Hash:
Alejandro1::ALEJANDRO:2a2740ee0e459eb3:773D05ADC2EC55F9BA44D3DC210FEADE:

3. Remediation & Mitigation
To secure the environment against this specific vector, the following measures are recommended:

Disable LLMNR/NetBIOS: Apply a Group Policy Object (GPO) to disable these legacy protocols throughout the domain.

Enforce SMB Signing: Require SMB Signing (at least "Required") to prevent SMB Relay attacks.

Network Segmentation: Restrict broadcast/multicast traffic between sensitive network segments.

Strong Password Policy: Implement a policy that enforces high-entropy passwords, making offline cracking attempts statistically unfeasible.
